title: Tag flags
tags: [[Hierarchical tags]]
kind: reference
aliases: partition always sparse
summary: Three bool fields on a tag entry — partition makes its children exclude one another, always and sparse steer the classification script.

A tag entry may carry three `bool` fields; one not written is `false`. The shipped
schema declares them with that default, so an entry created in the GUI shows them
([[Schema file]]) — in a repository created before them once `mf repo update-config`
merged the new default ([[Repository configuration]]); `mf tag` creates entries without them. `mf tag list` prints them
as three `0`/`1` columns after the uuid. Their names come from the `[tag]` table of
[[CLI configuration]].

|!Field |!On |!Effect |
|`partition` |a parent |its children exclude one another: `mf tag add` of one drops the others |
|`always` |any tag |`gui-tag-classify.sh` adds it without asking ([[Classifying with tags]]) |
|`sparse` |a parent |`gui-tag-classify.sh` picks its children by completion, one after another ([[Classifying with tags]]) |

!! Partition

`add P` drops, besides what subsumption drops ([[Tag subsumption]]), the `tag` refs to
the other children of every partition P is under, and to everything below them —
along each path of P, and for P's ancestors too: `musique/jazz/bebop` is jazz, so
under a partition `musique` it excludes `musique/rock` and `musique/rock/punk`. What P
implies is never dropped: a `fusion` under both `musique/jazz` and `musique/rock`
excludes neither. Only `add` excludes; `deny` and `mixed` are unchanged, and nothing
is written as a negative.

The flag is the parent's, so "its other children" names one set even when a child
has several parents ([[Why there is no exclusive flag]]).
